<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/11th-century-bce">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Revolt</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rebellion of the Three Guards</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1040 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century-bce">11th century BCE</a> · Also known as Rebellion of the Three Monitors</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Rebellion of the Three Guards was a revolt early in the Western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhou-dynasty">Zhou dynasty</a> by three brothers of King Wu, Guan Shu, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cai-shu-du">Cai Shu</a> and Huo Shu, who had been set to watch the conquered Shang. Allied with the Shang prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wu-geng">Wu Geng</a>, they rose against the regency of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duke-of-zhou">Duke of Zhou</a>, who crushed them after about three years and redistributed the Shang heartland among loyal kin.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rebellion-of-the-three-guards.jpg" alt="Rebellion of the Three Guards" width="320" height="229" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Philg88; derivative work by Applodion · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rebellion_of_the_Three_Guards.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/king-wu-of-zhou">King Wu of Zhou</a> took the Shang capital of Yin in 1046 BCE, most eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> stayed loyal to the fallen dynasty. Wu made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wu-geng">Wu Geng</a>, son of the last Shang king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/di-xin">Di Xin</a>, deputy ruler of the east, and set his brothers Shu Xian of Guan, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cai-shu-du">Shu Du of Cai</a> and Shu Chu of Huo to watch him. Wu died around 1043 BCE, and his brother Dan, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duke-of-zhou">Duke of Zhou</a>, declared himself regent for the young King Cheng, claiming, probably falsely, that the king was too young to rule.</p><p>Guanshu, who was older than Dan and by seniority had the better claim to the regency, suspected his brother of usurpation. The historian Li Feng notes that messages took forty to sixty days to cross the mountains of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henan">Henan</a>, breeding mistrust between the commanders in the east and the court at Fenghao. In 1042 BCE Guanshu and Caishu led <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wu-geng">Wu Geng</a> and his followers into revolt.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Huoshu joined his brothers, and large parts of the eastern realm rose, together with most of the Dongyi states of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shandong">Shandong</a> under Pugu and Yan and some <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huaiyi">Huaiyi</a> peoples, among them Xu. Song under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weizi">Weizi Qi</a>, Northern Yan and the Dongyi state of Xue stayed loyal and bore the fighting for almost a year while the court mobilised. The Dukes of Zhou and Shao then led a second eastern campaign, in which bronze inscriptions suggest King Cheng took part. In the second year Yin was destroyed and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wu-geng">Wu Geng</a> killed; Guanshu was executed, Huoshu reduced to a commoner and Caishu exiled. In the third year the royal army subdued the Huai peoples and finally took Yan.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duke-of-zhou">Duke of Zhou</a> reorganised the kingdom under the Fengjian system, granting most states to royal kin and loyal families, moving Shang nobles to distant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fiefs</a> and giving the Shang heartland to Wey under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kangshu-feng">Kangshu Feng</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weizi">Weizi Qi</a>. A second capital was built near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a>, and Zhou settlers colonised the east, beginning a conflict with its peoples that lasted until 771 BCE. The duke also expounded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mandate-of-heaven">Mandate of Heaven</a> to justify the new order. Later tradition, revering him, cast the brothers as evil men, though the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/three-kingdoms-period">Three Kingdoms</a> writer Ji Kang defended them. The poem Po Fu in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/classic-of-poetry">Classic of Poetry</a> honours the campaign.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Rebellion of the Three Guards” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Zhou at first left his son Wu Geng in charge of the Shang heartland under the supervision of King Wu's brothers, but after Wu's death Wu Geng joined the <b>Rebellion of the Three Guards</b> and was killed in the Duke of Zhou's campaign.</blockquote><a href="/empire/shang-dynasty">Shang dynasty · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The duke suppressed the <b>Rebellion of the Three Guards</b>, in which the Shang prince Wu Geng joined Wu's brothers Guanshu and Caishu, campaigned in the east for three years and built a second capital, Chengzhou, at Luoyi near modern Luoyang.</blockquote><a href="/empire/western-zhou">Western Zhou · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Cai Shu joined the rebellion of the <b>Three Guards</b> against the regency of the Duke of Zhou and was banished, but his son Cai Zhong was later restored to the fief.</blockquote><a href="/state/state-of-cai">State of Cai</a></li><li><blockquote>After the rebellion of the <b>Three Guards</b>, the Duke of Zhou gave the former Shang capital region around Zhaoge in northern Henan to Kang Shu Feng, a younger brother of King Wu, to watch over the conquered Shang population.</blockquote><a href="/state/state-of-wey">State of Wey</a></li></ul></section>
